Ladybird publishes Now Playing from its UI process, through
MPNowPlayingInfoCenter and MPRemoteCommandCenter, with the title,
artist, album, artwork, duration, elapsed time, and rate. Chromium’s
NowPlayingInfoCenterDelegateCocoa and Gecko’s
MediaHardwareKeysEventSourceMacMediaCenter publish the same way, from
their browser processes. WebKit publishes from its GPU process through
private MediaRemote calls (MediaSessionManagerCocoa::
setNowPlayingInfo()).

Ladybird enables play, pause, toggle, stop, next and previous track,
and change-playback-position from the session’s supported actions, and
leaves the skip and scanning commands off — the same set Chromium’s
RemoteCommandCenterDelegate enables. Gecko leaves stop off too.

These comparisons come from reading each engine’s source, at Chromium
8658d682eac5, WebKit 047a16c96ccd, and Gecko 06fa2674be3d: What an
engine hands the OS media controls isn’t observable from page script, so
it couldn’t be compared by running them.
